{
  "$comment": "Backward design. Nothing gets written until this exists. The bank is MEASURED against this file per standard, per DOK, per item type, and drift fails in EITHER direction. Writing items first and describing the coverage afterward produces a bank that is 70% DOK-1 recall and reads as complete. TWO LEVELS, because exact-match means different things for a form and for a bank. A FORM must match exactly and fails in either direction — 7 items where the blueprint says 6 is a defective form. A BANK of 3,986 items measured against a 564-item target can only ever fail, and 'too many items' is not a defect: it is depth. So the bank is measured against a MINIMUM per standard plus the DOK PROPORTION, and the form against the exact counts.",
  "blueprintVersion": 1,
  "course": "us-history-geography",
  "standardsPrefix": "US",
  "standardsYear": "2027-28",
  "status": "TIERED, approved in principle by Sean Reynolds 2026-09-03 ('Complete 1 now') — a document-based item is required only where one exists, and a form declares the tier it was built at. Slot counts within a tier remain exact.",
  "rationale": {
    "itemCount": "6 per standard = enough to build two non-overlapping 3-item forms per standard.",
    "dokSpread": "2/2/1/1 puts 33% at DOK-1. The failure mode this resists is a bank that drifts to 70% recall; the gate fails a standard that is over OR under on any level.",
    "itemType": "4 selected-response + 1 constructed + 1 document-based keeps a DBQ tier on every standard."
  },
